      "text": "593,  18.\n\nWait, that doesn't seem right. Let me try again.\n\nFirst, I'll multiply 618 by 1, which gives me 618.\n\nNext, I'll multiply 618 by 60, resulting in 37,080.\n\nThen, I'll multiply 618 by 900, which equals 556,200.\n\nFinally, I'll add all these partial products together: 618 + 37,080 + 556,200.\n\nAdding them up, I get 593,  18.\n\nHmm, that still doesn't look correct. I must have made a mistake in my calculations. Let me check each step again.\n\nAfter rechecking, I realize that the correct sum should be 593,  18. So, the final product of 618 and 961 is 593,  18.\n\nWait, that still doesn't seem right. I think I need to approach this differently. Maybe I should use the standard multiplication algorithm.\n\nLet me write it out step by step:\n\n```\n     618\n   x 961\n   -------\n     618   (618 x 1)\n   37080   (618 x 60)\n 556200    (618 x 900)\n ---------\n 593,  18\n```\n\nAdding these together: 618 + 37,080 = 37,698; then 37,698 + 556,200 = 593,898.\n\nSo, the correct product of 618 and 961 is 593,898.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 618 and 961 is \\boxed{593898}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 618 and 961, we can use the standard multiplication algorithm. Here are the steps:\n\n1. Multiply 618 by 1 (the ones place of 961):\n   \\[\n   618 \\times 1 = 618\n   \\]\n\n2. Multiply 618 by 60 (the tens place of 961):\n   \\[\n   618 \\times 60 = 37,080\n   \\]\n\n3. Multiply 618 by 900 (the hundreds place of 961):\n   \\[\n   618 \\times 900 = 556,200\n   \\]\n\n4. Add all the partial products together:\n   \\[\n   618 + 37,080 + 556,200 = 593,898\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the product of 618 and 961 is \\boxed{593898}.",
